speechtext:
1. Gymnasia: (a) Maximum Security, 12; (b) Medium Security, 11; (c) Minimum Security, nil; (d) Community Correctional Centres, nil (access to Y.M.C.A.); (e) Specialized (Prison for Women), 1. Swimming Pools: (a) Maximum Security, 1 (a small above-ground pool purchased by inmates at Archambault through their welfare fund); (b) Medium Security, nil (Community, swimming pools rented when possible); (c) Minimum Security, nil; (d) Community Correctional Centres nil; (e) Specialized (Prison for Women) nil. Other Facilities: (a) Maximum Security-All maximum institutions have fully equipped weight lifting rooms and hockey rinks. In the summertime, outdoor activities such as baseball, track and field, miniature golf and horseshoe pitching are organized, (b) Medium Security-Weight lifting rooms are provided in all medium institutions. Winter sports include the organization of hockey and broomball games. During the summer a whole range of outdoor activities are provided from track and field to baseball, (c) Minimum Security-During the winter months, hockey rinks are available and during the summer, depending on the location of the institution, activities range from baseball to swimming and fishing in near-by rivers, (d) Community Correctional Centres- These centres are located within various Canadian cities and they therefore rely entirely on community resources for recreational activities, (e) Specialized (Prison for Women)-Outdoor activities include the organization of baseball games and track and field events.
2. There are five qualified physical education instructors in the Canadian Penitentiary Service, located in the following institutions: Dorchester Penitentiary, 1; Saskatchewan Penitentiary, 1; Drumheller Institution, 3. In addition, the Penitentiary Service employs 12 Supervisors of Recreation. Three of these men are former Army Physical Training Instructors, two are former professional hockey players, two are former R.C.A.F. Recreational Specialists and the remaining five have on the job experience, have participated in community recreational programs had have attended various training courses. They are located in the following institutions: Matsqui Institution, Warkworth Institution; British Columbia Penitentiary, Cowansville Institution; Stony Mountain Institution, Archambault Institution; Reception Centre (Ontario), Leclerc Institution; Joyceville Institution, Correctional Development Centre; Collins Bay Institution, Springhill Institution. There are also 25 Recreational Officers in our institutions who have on the job experience and have attended training courses both inside and outside the institutions. They are located in the following institutions: Matsqui Institution, 1; British Columbia Penitentiary, 2; Saskatchewan Penitentiary, 2; Regional Reception Centre (Ontario), 1; Joyceville Institution, 2; Collins Bay Institution, 2; Millhaven Institution, 2; Federal Training Centre, 2; Cowansville Institution, 2; Archambault Institution, 3;

Leclerc Institution, 2; Dorchester Penitentiary, 2; Spring-hill Institution, 2.
